[["wrb.fr",null,"[null,[\"c_4a229b53ee3665af\",\"r_fa9aa890abe18744\"],null,null,[[\"rc_71bfa1a14437267a\",[\"```json\\n{\\n  \\\"tool_calls\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"name\\\": \\\"ipython\\\",\\n      \\\"arguments\\\": {\\n        \\\"code\\\": \\\"res \\u003d await bash(\\\\\\\"gh apiLet’s cut through the noise and get right into the code mechanics, because you are spot on: **you didn't customize anything**, and `luuquangvu` is either conflating two completely different timeouts or looking at high-level constants rather than the actual streaming loop in `client.py`.\\n\\nHere is the exact breakdown of why your log screamed `[Watchdog] Socket idle for 20s` and why his 120s claim is missing the plot:\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 1. Where the \\\"20 Seconds\\\" Actually Comes From\\n\\nIn reverse-engineered Gemini web clients (specifically in `_generate()` around line 1570 of `client.py`), there is a critical distinction between:\\n\\n1. **The Global Request/Session Budget:** The overarching request timeout (often defaulted to several minutes like 120s or 450s to allow massive generations to finish).\\n2. **The Socket Stall / Chunk Watchdog:** The delta timer inside the response stream loop. \\n\\nWhen consuming chunk deltas, the streaming watchdog checks how long the socket has been idle between packets. Because web sockets and chunked HTTP streams to Google's backend will silently hang or drop if a connection stalls, libraries enforce a **short inactivity threshold**—frequently hardcoded or defaulted to **15–20 seconds**—to detect dead pipes and trigger a refresh.\\n\\nThat is why your log explicitly printed:\\n```text\\n[Watchdog] Socket idle for 20s. Refreshing connection...\\n```\\nThe watchdog counter isn't pulling 20s out of thin air. It evaluated `stall_threshold` as 20s because of how the stream inactivity logic is configured by default during standard (non-thinking) chunk reads.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 2. Why `luuquangvu` Is Confused\\n\\n`luuquangvu` is conflating the **total silence tolerance** of an established request with the **per-chunk watchdog**:\\n\\n* **His assumption:** *\\\"The default watchdog is 120s, so the server has up to 2 minutes to respond.\\\"*\\n* **The reality in the code:** The 120s threshold only applies when the client *knows* a long delay is expected (e.g., when `is_thinking \\u003d\\u003d True`). But because `is_thinking` starts uninitialized as `False`, the loop assumes normal token streaming should have started immediately. \\n* Once normal streaming is assumed, the fallback stall threshold kicks in (the 20s inactivity check). When Gemini takes 25–35 seconds to ponder life, the universe, and everything on a heavy reasoning prompt before dropping its first delta token, the 20s timer expires and nukes the connection.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 3. The Smoking Gun: `is_thinking \\u003d False`\\n\\nThe bug you pointed out is an architectural state machine mismatch:\\n\\n```python\\n# What happens before the first chunk:\\nis_thinking \\u003d False  # \\u003c--- Starts False even if extended_thinking\\u003dTrue!\\n\\nfor chunk in response.iter_chunks():\\n    # Because is_thinking is False, stall_threshold drops to the tight 20s idle watchdog\\n    # instead of the full thinking grace period / request timeout.\\n    if socket_idle_time \\u003e stall_threshold:\\n        logger.warning(f\\\"[Watchdog] Socket idle for {stall_threshold}s. Refreshing connection...\\\")\\n        refresh_connection()\\n```\\n\\nBy the time the model actually finishes reasoning and prepares candidate chunks, the client has already severed the connection and attempted a session refresh.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### How to Answer His Comment\\n\\nHit him with the distinction between the global timeout and the inner loop's stall threshold so he doesn't stay stuck on the \\\"did you set a custom timeout?\\\" tangent:\\n\\n```markdown\\nHi @luuquangvu,\\n\\nNo custom timeouts or client configurations were applied—this is with standard defaults.\\n\\nThe 20s in the log originates from the inner chunk loop's stall detection logic (`stall_threshold`), not the top-level request timeout. \\n\\nHere is why it triggers at ~20s:\\n1. Before any candidate deltas arrive, `is_thinking` is initialized to `False` in `client.py:1570`.\\n2. Because `is_thinking` evaluates to `False`, the stream watchdog treats the pre-token phase as an idle/dead socket rather than an active reasoning window, falling back to the 20s stall limit.\\n3. Complex prompts with `extended_thinking\\u003dTrue` routinely require 25–40s of server-side computation before emitting the first delta. Because the client has not flagged that it is awaiting reasoning output, the 20s inactivity limit fires and severs the stream prematurely.\\n\\nInitializing `is_thinking \\u003d True if extended_thinking else False` at stream setup ensures the watchdog respects the full timeout window until the first delta updates the state.\\n\"],null,null,null,null,null,null,[1],\"en\",null,null,[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,[]],null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,[],null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,[]]],[\"New York, USA\",\"SWML_DESCRIPTION_FROM_YOUR_INTERNET_ADDRESS\",false,null,\"//www.google.com/maps/vt/data\\u003dAvIzW62PGhAfUQRpZ9e4q2af_7_WOsFiVpXkQpd9LX0zB0dpJdp9NdkGa-o9tBoMCvd_Di6NgmlfyQXVg4MjqFFJU5C9KoCTOCJ6dgPFDBPpFVbqYZh0Zc64U3skCrf4jwF8niK7flGYwqgOmji5U7TBRwp7BHZh3ps424DbYR5MR0cpmx2J9OED3SQgECsR\"],null,null,\"US\",null,null,null,null,null,false,null,null,null,null,\"en\",null,null,null,true,[null,[false,false,true],[false,true,false,false,true,false],false,[[[\"Academic - Other\",2.8056465E-4],[\"Coding\",0.008082441],[\"Creative - Other\",6.894236E-4],[\"Creative Writing\",5.874187E-4],[\"Customer Support\",8.409637E-6],[\"Daily Life Expert - Other\",8.733169E-4],[\"Daily Life Personal - Other\",7.0075487E-4],[\"Data Analysis\",0.0013721668],[\"Emotional Support\",6.61969E-4],[\"Engagement - Other\",0.0065164305],[\"Ev